As with the passive voice, it's often forgotten that the rule is not
"never use" but "avoid."

I forget the details, but there was a point in my dissertation work
where I was adjusting two parallel sets of code (sometimes blowing it),
which were sometimes used for initialization, depending upon how the
function was entered (massive number pounding dynamic programming, with
prior calculations cached).

I felt like a complete idiot when I realized *just* how much work I'd
engage in to avoid a GOTO . . .

The states that were being calculated could possibly be first,
intermediate, or final generation (it was a breeding problem),with both
first & last being "special cases", so there were multiple entrances &
exits.

But I did develop a method that solved what we thought was a 60
dimensional problem (it was actually higher than that; choices in early
dimensions could determine partially or fully disjoint latter dimensions
. . .).  For our baseline 15 generation problem with four continuous
choice variables per generaion, the first generation alonge produced a
thirtieth order multinomial in sixty unknowns--well, sort of--each of
those multinomial terms multiplied a psychotic function using the same
variables in both both the CDF and inverse CDF with those variables,
dropping by two orders and four unknowns for each generation.

Hmm, I'm *really* rambling :)

Anyway, there are times and places for GOTO, where you need a true
abomination to avoid them--but they're rare.  That's the only one I
recall using since microsoft changed BASIC-80 so that it didn't have to
search memory for line numbers every time (mbasic5?).  Before that, your
first line would be a goto to a high line number where the program
started, leaving your subroutines in lower memory.

